(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["cam-nutrition-exam-1-pregnancy-breastfeeding-quiz-version-2"]={"title":"Pregnancy & Breastfeeding (Set 2) — CAM/Nutrition Exam 1","category":"CAM / Nutrition","exam":"Exam 1","sem":"summer-1-2026","questions":[{"q":"According to the lecture's general pregnancy recommendations, how many cups of water per day should a pregnant woman consume, unless contraindicated?","choices":["8 to 12 cups (64 to 96 ounces)","4 to 6 cups (32 to 48 ounces)","14 to 16 cups (112 to 128 ounces)","3 to 4 liters, but only during the third trimester"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct -- the lecture lists this as the recommended daily water intake during pregnancy, unless contraindicated. — Nutrition Pregnancy Breastfeeding 2026 Dr. McInnis.pptx, Slide 4"},{"q":"A woman with pre-existing diabetes is counseled to normalize her blood glucose before conception because organogenesis, the period when major fetal organs form, occurs during which weeks of embryonic development?","choices":["Weeks 1-2","Weeks 3-8","Weeks 9-12","Weeks 20-24"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct -- the lecture states organogenesis occurs during weeks 3-8 of embryonic development, when maternal hyperglycemia raises the risk of congenital malformations. — Nutrition Pregnancy Breastfeeding 2026 Dr. McInnis.pptx, Slide 6"},{"q":"Which of the following is listed in the lecture as an effect of maternal obesity during the preconception and pregnancy period?","choices":["Increased fertility and likelihood of conception","Decreased risk of cesarean delivery","Increased postpartum weight retention","Decreased long-term health risks for offspring"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct -- the lecture lists increased postpartum weight retention as an effect of maternal obesity. — Nutrition Pregnancy Breastfeeding 2026 Dr. McInnis.pptx, Slide 7"},{"q":"A neural tube defect in which major parts of the brain and skull fail to develop is known as:","choices":["Spina bifida","Encephalocele","Meningocele","Anencephaly"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct -- the lecture defines anencephaly as the condition in which major parts of the brain and skull do not develop. — Nutrition Pregnancy Breastfeeding 2026 Dr. McInnis.pptx, Slide 8"},{"q":"Per the lecture's definition, a 36-year-old woman would be classified as experiencing infertility after how many months of unprotected intercourse without conception?","choices":["6 months","12 months","3 months","18 months"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct -- the lecture defines infertility as inability to conceive after 6 months for women 35 years or older, and this patient is 36. — Nutrition Pregnancy Breastfeeding 2026 Dr. McInnis.pptx, Slide 9"},{"q":"According to the lecture, low body fat in males is associated with which of the following?","choices":["Increased sperm count","Decreased sex drive and decreased sperm count","Increased estrogen levels","Threefold higher infertility risk compared to normal-weight men"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct -- the lecture lists decreased sex drive and decreased sperm count as effects of low body fat in males. — Nutrition Pregnancy Breastfeeding 2026 Dr. McInnis.pptx, Slide 10"},{"q":"Per the lecture, maternal dietary restrictions during pregnancy increase the risk of which outcome?","choices":["Fetal macrosomia risk","Gestational diabetes","Low fetal birth weight","Childhood obesity"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct -- the lecture lists poor fetal development and low fetal birth weight as risks of maternal dietary restrictions. — Nutrition Pregnancy Breastfeeding 2026 Dr. McInnis.pptx, Slide 11"},{"q":"A pregnant patient reports craving and eating clay and ice. This behavior, known as pica, may be associated with which underlying condition per the lecture?","choices":["Vitamin A toxicity","Gestational diabetes","Folate deficiency","Iron deficiency"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct -- the lecture states pica may be associated with iron deficiency. — Nutrition Pregnancy Breastfeeding 2026 Dr. McInnis.pptx, Slide 13"},{"q":"The lecture describes the third trimester as a critical time for which nutrient, noting that the fetus takes priority for it?","choices":["Iron","Folic acid","Vitamin D","Calcium"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct -- the lecture states the third trimester is a critical time for iron, as the fetus takes priority for it. — Nutrition Pregnancy Breastfeeding 2026 Dr. McInnis.pptx, Slide 15"},{"q":"Based on the Dutch Famine research described in the lecture, which increased health risk was linked specifically to famine exposure during the first trimester, and not listed for the second or third trimester?","choices":["Kidney disease","Coronary heart disease","Obstructive lung disease","Glucose intolerance"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct -- the lecture lists coronary heart disease under first-trimester exposure, along with an atherosclerosis-associated lipid profile, stress sensitivity, obesity, and breast cancer. — Nutrition Pregnancy Breastfeeding 2026 Dr. McInnis.pptx, Slide 16"},{"q":"Which increased health risk did the Dutch Famine research, as summarized in the lecture, associate with exposure specifically during the second trimester of gestation?","choices":["Breast cancer","Stress sensitivity","Obstructive lung disease","Blood lipid profile associated with atherosclerosis"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct -- the lecture lists obstructive lung disease under second-trimester exposure, along with kidney disease and glucose intolerance. — Nutrition Pregnancy Breastfeeding 2026 Dr. McInnis.pptx, Slide 16"},{"q":"An infant born whose fetal weight falls below the 10th percentile for gestational age would be classified as:","choices":["Macrosomic for gestational age","Classified as low birth weight","Considered a post-term infant","Small for gestational age (SGA)"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct -- the lecture defines SGA as fetal weight below the 10th percentile for gestational age. — Nutrition Pregnancy Breastfeeding 2026 Dr. McInnis.pptx, Slide 18"},{"q":"Regarding protein intake during pregnancy, the lecture notes a potential downside to relying heavily on animal protein sources. What is it?","choices":["Many animal protein sources are high in fat, which may contribute to excessive weight gain","Animal protein sources are said to have universally poor bioavailability","Animal protein sources provide less than 10% of the recommended daily protein","Routine protein supplementation is specifically recommended to offset this risk"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct -- the lecture states many animal protein sources are high in fat and may contribute to excessive weight gain. — Nutrition Pregnancy Breastfeeding 2026 Dr. McInnis.pptx, Slide 20"},{"q":"During the second half of pregnancy, what happens to plasma lipids according to the lecture?","choices":["Plasma cholesterol and triglycerides both decrease noticeably","Plasma cholesterol increases about 50% and triglycerides may triple","Only plasma triglycerides rise; cholesterol stays stable","Plasma lipids remain unchanged until after delivery"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct -- the lecture states plasma cholesterol increases roughly 50% and plasma triglycerides may triple during the second half of pregnancy. — Nutrition Pregnancy Breastfeeding 2026 Dr. McInnis.pptx, Slide 21"},{"q":"Per the ACOG recommendations cited in the lecture, what is the recommended folic acid dose once a woman of average risk is already pregnant?","choices":["0.4 mg","4 mg","0.6 mg","2.6 mcg"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct -- the lecture states ACOG recommends 0.6 mg of folic acid during pregnancy. — Nutrition Pregnancy Breastfeeding 2026 Dr. McInnis.pptx, Slide 24"},{"q":"Which type of omega-3 fatty acid does the lecture identify as plant-based, in contrast to the fish-oil-derived types?","choices":["Docosahexaenoic acid (DHA)","Eicosapentaenoic acid (EPA)","Arachidonic acid","Alpha-linolenic acid"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct -- the lecture identifies alpha-linolenic acid as the plant-based omega-3 fatty acid. — Nutrition Pregnancy Breastfeeding 2026 Dr. McInnis.pptx, Slide 25"},{"q":"Why does the lecture specifically caution about iodine status during pregnancy, beyond simply stating its RDA?","choices":["Most prenatal vitamins do not contain much, if any, iodine","Iodine is toxic in any amount during pregnancy","Iodine RDA decreases during pregnancy compared to non-pregnant adults","Iodine is only obtainable through supplementation"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct -- the lecture specifically notes that most prenatal vitamins do not contain much, if any, iodine, despite its importance. — Nutrition Pregnancy Breastfeeding 2026 Dr. McInnis.pptx, Slide 27"},{"q":"Why does the lecture specifically advise pregnant women to avoid eating liver, especially during the first trimester?","choices":["Liver is a common source of foodborne listeria contamination risk","Liver is very high in vitamin A, and excessive intake increases the risk of a teratogenic effect","Liver is known to contain unsafe, elevated levels of mercury","Liver is thought to interfere significantly with normal dietary iron absorption"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct -- the lecture states excessive vitamin A intake increases teratogenic risk and specifically advises avoiding liver, especially in the first trimester. — Nutrition Pregnancy Breastfeeding 2026 Dr. McInnis.pptx, Slide 29"},{"q":"According to the lecture's patient education on caloric requirements, how many additional kcal/day are recommended during the second trimester?","choices":["No additional calories","+450 kcal/day","+340 kcal/day","+680 kcal/day"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct -- the lecture states the second trimester recommendation is an additional 340 kcal/day. — Nutrition Pregnancy Breastfeeding 2026 Dr. McInnis.pptx, Slide 34"},{"q":"Per the lecture's weight gain pattern, approximately how much weight should be gained per week during the second and third trimesters?","choices":["About 1-5 pounds total for both trimesters combined","No additional weight gain is expected until the third trimester","About 1 pound every 2 weeks","About 1 pound per week"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct -- the lecture states approximately 1 pound per week is gained during the second and third trimesters. — Nutrition Pregnancy Breastfeeding 2026 Dr. McInnis.pptx, Slide 36"},{"q":"On physical exam, a pregnant patient has enlarged parotid glands and eroded tooth enamel. Per the lecture, these findings are associated with which eating disorder?","choices":["Bulimia","Anorexia","Binge eating disorder","Pica"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct -- the lecture lists enlarged parotid glands and eroded tooth enamel as physical exam signs associated with bulimia. — Nutrition Pregnancy Breastfeeding 2026 Dr. McInnis.pptx, Slide 39"},{"q":"According to the lecture's fish consumption guidelines, what is defined as one serving of fish for a pregnant woman?","choices":["2 ounces","4 ounces","6 ounces","8 ounces"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct -- the lecture defines a serving of fish as 4 ounces. — Nutrition Pregnancy Breastfeeding 2026 Dr. McInnis.pptx, Slide 41"},{"q":"According to the ACOG guidance cited in the lecture, what daily caffeine intake does not appear to be a major contributing factor to miscarriage or preterm birth?","choices":["Any amount, since caffeine has no known effects in pregnancy","Less than 500 mg/day","Less than 200 mg/day","Caffeine should be avoided completely, with zero tolerance"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct -- the lecture states ACOG considers moderate caffeine consumption of less than 200 mg/day not to be a major contributing factor to miscarriage or preterm birth. — Nutrition Pregnancy Breastfeeding 2026 Dr. McInnis.pptx, Slide 42"},{"q":"Which of the following herbal supplements does the lecture specifically flag as a concern during pregnancy?","choices":["Ginger","Pyridoxine (vitamin B6)","Peppermint","Black cohosh"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct -- the lecture lists black cohosh among the herbal supplements of particular concern during pregnancy. — Nutrition Pregnancy Breastfeeding 2026 Dr. McInnis.pptx, Slide 43"},{"q":"Per the lecture, adolescent pregnancy (under age 20) carries an increased risk for which of the following outcomes?","choices":["Preeclampsia-eclampsia, prematurity, and small for gestational age infants","Fetal aneuploidy and gestational diabetes risk","Macrosomia and increased cesarean delivery rate","Obstructive lung disease appearing later in the offspring"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct -- the lecture lists these as increased risks associated with adolescent (under 20) pregnancy. — Nutrition Pregnancy Breastfeeding 2026 Dr. McInnis.pptx, Slide 44"},{"q":"Which hormone is responsible for the 'let-down reflex,' the ejection or release of breastmilk?","choices":["Prolactin","Oxytocin","Estrogen","Progesterone"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct -- the lecture states oxytocin promotes breastmilk ejection or release, known as the let-down reflex. — Nutrition Pregnancy Breastfeeding 2026 Dr. McInnis.pptx, Slide 52"},{"q":"Human milk oligosaccharides (HMOs) found in colostrum promote the growth of which type of beneficial intestinal bacteria, per the lecture?","choices":["Lactobacillus","Escherichia coli","Bifidobacteria","Streptococcus"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct -- the lecture states HMOs in colostrum promote growth of beneficial intestinal bacteria, particularly Bifidobacteria. — Nutrition Pregnancy Breastfeeding 2026 Dr. McInnis.pptx, Slide 55"},{"q":"Hindmilk, released later in a feeding, differs from foremilk in that it:","choices":["Contains more water and resembles skim milk","Is only produced during the colostrum stage","Is released within the first 3 to 5 minutes of feeding","Resembles cream, being released later in the feeding"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct -- the lecture states hindmilk, released after about 10 to 20 minutes of feeding, resembles cream. — Nutrition Pregnancy Breastfeeding 2026 Dr. McInnis.pptx, Slide 58"},{"q":"Per the lecture, breastfeeding is associated with which long-term benefit for the infant?","choices":["Reduced risk of obesity later in life and modest improvements in cognitive performance","Accelerated uterine involution occurring after delivery of the placenta","Protection against maternal breast and ovarian cancer","Delayed return of the mother's normal ovulatory menstrual cycles"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct -- the lecture lists reduced risk of obesity later in life and modest cognitive performance improvements among the infant advantages of breastfeeding. — Nutrition Pregnancy Breastfeeding 2026 Dr. McInnis.pptx, Slide 61"},{"q":"Once breastfeeding is well established, a well-nourished infant's stool should typically appear:","choices":["Chalky and firm","Lumpy, mustard-appearing","Watery and clear","Thick and black (meconium) beyond the first week"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct -- the lecture states a well-nourished breastfed infant should have lumpy, mustard-appearing stool. — Nutrition Pregnancy Breastfeeding 2026 Dr. McInnis.pptx, Slide 62"}]};
